Setup. Fix a positive integer d. For a measurable function (Fourier kernel) A: ℝ^d × ℝ^d → ℂ and a "symbol" b on ℝ^d, the scalar paracommutator T_b(A) is the bilinear-type operator densely defined on Schwartz functions f by

    (T_b(A) f)\^(ξ) = ∫_{ℝ^d} A(ξ, η) \hat b(ξ − η) \hat f(η) dη,

where \^ denotes the Fourier transform. In the scalar theory one has known characterizations (in terms of function-space membership of b, e.g. BMO, Besov spaces B_p^{s,q}, etc., and structural hypotheses on A) of:
 (i) boundedness of T_b(A) on L^2(ℝ^d);
 (ii) compactness of T_b(A) on L^2(ℝ^d);
 (iii) membership of T_b(A) in the Schatten–von Neumann ideal S_p, 0 < p ≤ ∞.

Problem. Let H_1, H_2 be separable Hilbert spaces. Consider the vector- or operator-valued paracommutator T_b(A) defined analogously, where now the kernel A takes values in the bounded operators B(H_1, H_2) and/or the symbol b is a function on ℝ^d with values in B(H_1, H_2) (or in a suitable operator ideal). Develop a general theory for such vector-valued and operator-valued paracommutators that parallels the scalar theory: in particular, formulate and prove necessary and sufficient conditions on the pair (A, b), expressed in terms of natural vector- or operator-valued function spaces (e.g. operator-valued BMO and Besov spaces), characterizing

 (a) the boundedness of T_b(A): L^2(ℝ^d; H_1) → L^2(ℝ^d; H_2);
 (b) the compactness of T_b(A);
 (c) the membership of T_b(A) in the Schatten–von Neumann classes S_p, 0 < p ≤ ∞,

so that the resulting theory recovers the scalar results when H_1 = H_2 = ℂ and applies, in particular, to "big" Hankel operators viewed as vectorial paracommutators.

